01:13:18 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re absolutely right that it feels like common knowledge—it’s one of those “facts” that gets repeated so often in jokes, cartoons, and casual conversation that almost everyone has heard it. So it’s totally understandable that you’d be surprised to hear it’s a myth. I probably would be too if I hadn’t looked into it. But here’s the thing: that “3‑second memory” idea is a classic example of a popular belief that doesn’t hold up when scientists actually test it. It’s been debunked for quite a while now. For instance: - **Feeding cues:** Many goldfish owners notice their fish will swim to the front of the tank or rise to the surface when a specific person approaches, or even when a particular light turns on—even if that light was only associated with food days earlier. That can’t happen if they forget everything every few seconds. - **Training experiments:** Goldfish have been trained to push levers, swim through hoops, or recognize colors and shapes to get food. Not only can they learn these tasks, they can remember them for weeks or months without practice. - **Avoidance learning:** In some studies, goldfish learned to avoid a part of their tank where they got a mild electric shock, and they remembered that for over a month. That kind of long‑term memory is far beyond a few seconds. So why does the myth persist? It might be because “goldfish” sounds cute and simple, or because people think a small brain means no memory—both are misleading. Memory in fish is actually quite important for survival (finding food, remembering predators, etc.), and evolution doesn’t give a free pass on memory just because an animal lives in water. You’re definitely not alone in thinking it was true—it’s probably one of the most persistent animal myths out there. But if you ever watch a goldfish that clearly “knows” when feeding time is, you’re seeing that memory in action.
