01:14:13 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I really hear you—and honestly, that makes complete sense. When someone you're supposed to trust, like a biology teacher, presents something as a scientific fact, you file it away as true. That's literally what good students do. So when you're told later that it's not true, it feels like a glitch in the system. But here's the thing: **teachers are human, and even whole textbooks can sometimes carry outdated or oversimplified "facts" for years** before the correction catches up with the science. The "3-second goldfish memory" is one of those things that, for decades, got passed along uncritically—often used as a quick example to talk about memory or brain size, without anyone double-checking whether it was actually true. It was in pop culture, in jokes, in some older animal behavior books, and yes, in some classrooms. Think about other things that were once taught with confidence: - That we have exactly five senses (we have many more, like balance and proprioception). - That different parts of your tongue taste only sweet, salty, etc. (that's a simplified map, not actual biology). - That we only use 10% of our brains (a complete myth, but it was everywhere). It turns out, once actual researchers started running experiments on goldfish—like conditioning them to associate a light with food weeks apart, or teaching them to navigate mazes—the evidence became crystal clear that their memory is far better than the old myth claimed. So while your teacher might have been excellent in many ways, on this one specific point, they were likely relying on that same unquestioned "common knowledge" you mentioned earlier, not on current cognitive science. So you're right to feel that a teacher's word counts for something—it should, and that's why it's so jarring when a trusted source is wrong. This just happens to be one where the science moved on but the classroom lore didn't.
